<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Belfort Gap</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Burgundian Gate</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Belfort Gap, or Burgundian Gate, is a lowland pass in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a> to the north and the Jura to the south, linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> valley with the basins of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a>. Guarded by Montbéliard and later by the fortress of Belfort, it has been a major route of trade and invasion and carries the Rhine–Rhône canal and modern road and rail links.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/belfort-gap.jpg" alt="Belfort Gap" width="320" height="165" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photographie : ALLIOT Brigitte · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vue_sur_l%27%C3%A9tang_des_Forges_(Belfort_-_Offemont).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
